      "id": 1,
      "text": "Settlers arrived in 1668, founding Shaftesbury Precinct in Albemarle County; it became Chowan Precinct in 1681, named after the Chowan River and its indigenous tribe.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/cape_colony_north_carolina",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Tidal surges from the Chowan River can flood Cape Colony during high tides, storms, or heavy rain, altering water salinity and sediment.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/cape_colony_north_carolina",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Before Europeans, the Chowanoke, an Algonquian tribe, farmed and lived along the Chowan River near what is now Cape Colony.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/cape_colony_north_carolina",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "In the 18th and 19th centuries, Cape Colony’s economy relied on tobacco farming, pine naval stores, and river fishing.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19,
        20,
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/cape_colony_north_carolina",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "By the mid‑20th century, Cape Colony transformed into a residential suburb southeast of Edenton.",
